import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import VivTerm from '../components/VivTerm.jsx';
import programs from '../data/programs.json';

const files = [
  ...programs.programs.map((p) => ({ name: p.file, url: `/programs/examples/${p.file}` })),
  ...programs.std.map((f) => ({ name: f, url: `/programs/examples/${f}` })),
];
---

<Base
  title="Terminal · Viv"
  description="The Viv CLI in your browser: run, c, wasm, features, doc, whatis, lsfunc, lsvar, src, deps, canon, hash, diff — the same functions the installed binary calls, on the example programs from the repo."
  eyebrow="Terminal"
  heading="The CLI, with nothing installed">
  <p class="lede">
    Each command here calls the same library function as the installed <code>viv</code> binary:
    <code>main.rs</code> only parses the command line, and the wasm module exports the functions it
    calls. So <code>hash</code>, <code>diff</code>, <code>canon</code> and the rest print byte for
    byte what the installed compiler prints on the same file. The terminal has
    {programs.programs.length + programs.std.length} files loaded: the
    {programs.programs.length} <a href="/examples">example programs</a> and the
    {programs.std.length} files of the standard library.
  </p>

  <div class="cellhost cellhost-wide">
    <VivTerm client:load files={files} />
  </div>

  <h2>Four commands to try first</h2>
  <dl class="deflist">
    <dt>hash control.viv</dt>
    <dd>
      Per-definition content addresses: a node hash (name included) and a body hash (the code
      alone). Reformat a definition and neither moves; rename it and only the node hash does.
      Because the body hash ignores the name, a diff can report a rename without guessing.
    </dd>
    <dt>canon scf_water.viv</dt>
    <dd>
      The program reordered into canonical dependency order, which is idempotent — run it on its
      own output and nothing moves. Try it on this 400-line Hartree–Fock program.
    </dd>
    <dt>features</dt>
    <dd>
      What Viv can do, by area: syntax, types, arrays, autodiff, correctness, the lenses, tools,
      performance, libraries, and the builtins, phrases and modules generated from the compiler's
      own tables. <code>features value-and-grad</code> shows one entry: what it does, when to use
      it, and a minimal example and its output; <code>features --search sort</code>
      finds entries by words. The installed binary adds <code>viv features --usage</code>, which
      counts how often a corpus uses each feature and lists code that does by hand what a feature
      does.
    </dd>
    <dt>doc fem_poisson.viv</dt>
    <dd>
      The structured annotations as JSON: per-definition what/how/why, tags, and the variable
      glossary. It is the JSON a code browser
      renders.
    </dd>
  </dl>

  <p class="note">
    <code>wasm &lt;file&gt;</code> compiles the file with the direct wasm backend, without C, and
    runs the module in your browser. It prints what <code>run</code> prints.
  </p>
</Base>
